Subject: [PATCH 08002/11362] [outer-loop] HUD mask + symmetric change gate +
 hidden-state closure, all DEFAULT-OFF -- and the first mask measurement had
 measured mask-OFF twice
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

User instruction: "fix the hud mask and the trust gate", after the induction-quality
diagnosis (trust median 0.0 vs a 0.5 gate, planned=0 on 174/174 rows) and after the
leaderboard technique watch showed the #9 GENERAL-PURPOSE contender feeding "object
segmentation with translation-invariant hashes ... NOT the raw numeric grid" and
"explicitly rejects changing border strips as likely HUD/timer state" -- both of
these fixes, arrived at independently by a contender scoring ~20x our 0.08.

THREE MECHANISMS, EACH BEHIND ITS OWN DEFAULT-OFF FLAG so their effects can never
confound (the two fixes push in OPPOSITE directions -- masking REMOVES unattainable
cells and should RAISE accuracy; the gate REJECTS degenerates and should LOWER the
pass rate; shipped together a null would be uninterpretable):
  * REQ-ARC-WMTE-6010  SUBMITTED_WORLD_MODEL_HUD_MASK_ENABLED = False
  * REQ-ARC-WMTE-6011  SUBMITTED_WORLD_MODEL_CHANGE_GATE_ENABLED = False
  * REQ-ARC-WMTE-6013  SUBMITTED_WORLD_MODEL_CHANGE_GATE_HIDDEN_STATE_ENABLED = None
All three read via `_flag_env`, so an arm is selected by ENV, never by editing a
constant. NOTHING IS FLIPPED -- the flip is the operator's call.

THE DEFECT THAT MATTERS MOST: THE FIRST MASK MEASUREMENT MEASURED MASK-OFF TWICE.
`WorldModelVerifier` discarded an unflagged mask while
`score_change_weighted_consistency` applied one unconditionally -- two comparators
with two meanings of "the same state", inside one decision whose docstring PROMISES
they "move TOGETHER". Measured: `change_fidelity` differed between mask arms on
0 of 162 paired arms (`hud_mask_status: disabled` on every mask=1 row) while
`incumbent_consistency` differed on 9/162. Fixed via one resolver; the mask now
applies on 111/162 and moves fidelity on 29. NOTE FOR THE RECORD: the four-arm
matrix was designed specifically to prevent confounding and did NOT catch this --
the adversarial review did. The confound was INSIDE an arm, not between arms.

THE MASK IS NOT UNIFORMLY SAFE -- ON TWO GAMES IT DELETES THE GAME, NOT THE CHROME.
Changed-cell overlap: lf52 1.0000 (60 changing transitions -> 0), su15 0.7568
(28 -> 1), every other game 0.0000-0.2219. On lf52 the corpus becomes DYNAMICS-FREE,
so the IDENTITY ENGINE IS OPTIMAL -- the concrete mechanism behind metric laundering.
REQ-ARC-WMTE-6015 adds a REFUSE-ON-DOUBT swallow guard that fires on exactly
lf52+su15 and on NONE of the other 15, recording `hud_mask_status:
refused_swallows_dynamics` rather than silently masking. Consequence the operator
must weigh: the mask is a PER-GAME intervention, not a global switch.

THE GATE: THE GAP FILE'S OWN PROPOSED FIX WOULD HAVE REJECTED CORRECT MODELS.
GAP-WM-TRUST-GATE proposed `change_accuracy >= T AND n_changes_correct >= k` and
instructed that it "needs adversarial review before landing". That review ran. With
a hand-written genuinely-predictive ls20 engine (5x5 sprite, 4-dir, step 5, moves
iff destination is background), 10 seeds, n=150 matched: the CURRENT gate rejects it
10/10; the GAP'S OWN `change_accuracy` scores 0.0000 and ALSO rejects it 10/10;
symmetric-union fidelity scores 0.846-0.912 against identity at EXACTLY 0.0000,
10/10, min reachable two-sided p 0.00195. Implementing the gap as written would have
shipped a gate that rejects correct models.
The metric: with M = (g0 != g1) true-change and P = (g0 != pred) engine-wrote,
`sym_change = |{c in M u P : pred[c] == g1[c]}| / |M u P|`. A missed change is wrong
AND an INVENTED write is wrong -- `cell_recall` masks to true changes only and so
CANNOT SEE an invented cell. Independently confirmed on the shipped code: IDENTITY
(writes nothing) 0.0000 REJECT; PERFECT 1.0000 PASS; CLOBBER (writes 9 over the
whole grid) cell_recall 1.000 but change_fidelity 0.0156 REJECT. Both degenerate
directions closed. T=0.5 sits mid-band: all 24 on-disk engines <= 0.085, identity
exactly 0.0, the real engine >= 0.846.

THE COVERAGE HOLE, FOUND AND CLOSED (REQ-6013). `change_gate_decision` had exactly
ONE call site, in the non-hidden-state branch. The OTHER branch -- taken for the 11
HIDDEN_STATE_GAME_IDS, which include EVERY ONE of the 0.08-wall games
(cn04/ar25/sc25/sk48/wa30) -- admitted on `trust_pass` and never called it. No unit
test could have caught it: a gate can be arithmetically perfect and never consulted.
It now computes INSIDE `select_trusted_world_model`, on the held-out split that
function owns, and REPLACES `trust_pass` rather than AND-ing (the incumbent is wrong
in BOTH directions -- blind to a spurious writer on 31/33 rows, rejects the honest
engine on 2/3 -- so a conjunct would import the false-reject and make a null
unattributable). Candidate RANKING untouched.

GUARDS FIRE ON THEIR ORIGIN INCIDENT, WITH HALF THE EVIDENCE DISCLOSED AS WORTHLESS.
Real on-disk lp85 rejected 6/6 arms through the PRODUCTION path over 7-9 real
changing transitions. ft09's rejection is classified VACUOUS and explicitly does NOT
support the claim: its held-out split has 0 of 40 changing transitions, so every
engine including a perfect one is rejected there -- counting it would have been a
pass that could not have failed. Must-not-fire control: a hand-written dc22 engine
ADMITTED 3/3 seeds under both mask settings. 62 of 68 arms closed; all 6 escapes are
re86 and all 6 are exactly the rows where `n_noop == 0`, and the acceptance gate
FAILS if any escape lacks that witness.

MUTATION PROOFS 10/10, AND THE TWO INITIAL MISSES WERE REAL DEFECTS: `if _hs_gate_on:`
-> `if False:` left the gate present-but-DEAD with the suite green (the structural
test now rejects a CONSTANT guard, not just a missing one); and `union = m | wrote`
-> recall-only was invisible because the attack also tripped the no-op channel -- a
test named "the symmetric gate rejects the invented write" was passing for a reason
other than its name. Two new no-op-free tests isolate the union metric, one of which
PINS the known limitation that it catches a heavy writer but not a light one.

TWO REVIEW FINDINGS WERE WRONG AND WERE ARGUED DOWN, NOT COMPLIED WITH. "ft09's
rejection is vacuous" reasoned from the documented `accuracy >= 0.5`; at the LIVE
`min_heldout_accuracy=1.0` ft09's identity engine scores a PERFECT 1.0 and is
ADMITTED, making it the SHARPEST origin case. Both thresholds now emit with the
threshold named in the key. "MAX_ACTIONS is 200 not 400" is half right: both exist
(:117 module-scope 200; :6501 CarnotAgent 400, the operator-settled override).

WHAT THIS DOES *NOT* SHOW, stated plainly: the LIVE four-arm A/B NEVER RAN. Every
result here is OFFLINE DECISION-LEVEL -- it shows the gate admits and rejects the
right engines, NOT that the live win rate moves. No episode was played and no level
solved. The live measurement remains owed and nothing here substitutes for it.

FRESHNESS: 7 artifacts rebuilt serially and ALONE (concurrent same-module rebuilds
corrupted an artifact earlier today). Diffed against before-images: ONE changed value
across all seven, a wall-clock string inside prose (72.253s -> 62.899s) for the SAME
18/18 cells re-captured -- which incidentally re-confirms the behavioural-neutrality
evidence it sits inside. Zero measurement-bearing moves; every verdict identical.

TWO REBUILD ERRORS I MADE AND CAUGHT, both by diffing rather than by trusting a
green lint:

1. WRONG ORDER. I rebuilt 7 artifacts and THEN ran `ruff format` on the two modules
   they depend on, which immediately re-staled all 7 plus 4 more. "Format first, THEN
   rebuild" is a lesson an earlier agent in this session already wrote down after
   hitting it, and I inverted it anyway. Redone in the right order.

2. WRONG DEPENDENCY ORDER, AND IT BROKE PROVENANCE. I ran the LIVE captures LAST, but
   `gateway_accurate_rescore` CONSUMES `per_level_reset_attribution` -- so it was built
   against a stale input. Worse: rebuilding exp6011/6012/6013 without
   `CARNOT_ARC_E3_DIR=$PWD/results/arc_e3_origin_fixtures` silently flipped
   `engine_store_is_frozen_fixtures` True -> False and repointed `engine_store` from the
   FROZEN fixture snapshot to the LIVE `results/arc_e3`. The verdicts happened to match,
   so nothing looked wrong -- but the measurement's reproducibility guarantee was gone,
   and those fixtures exist precisely because live engine regeneration had already
   contaminated this run once. The scripts record their own rebuild command including
   that env var; I omitted it. Rebuilt correctly; all three now report frozen=True.

3. I REFORMATTED THE FROZEN EVIDENCE. Chasing the format hook I ran
   `ruff check --fix` + `ruff format` over "every changed .py", which included all 27
   `results/arc_e3_origin_fixtures/*/world_model.py` -- the BYTE-PRESERVED snapshots of
   LLM-generated engines that exp6011/6012/6013 cite as proof the gate rejects REAL
   degenerates rather than synthetic stand-ins. It stripped `import numpy as np` from
   ft09's engine and removed unused locals in cn04/lp85/vc33: a silent rewrite of the
   artifact those experiments depend on. Recovered from the git index (they were staged
   pre-format, so the index held the original bytes) and verified all 27 back to `A `.
   ROOT CAUSE + FIX: pyproject's `extend-exclude` covered `results/arc_e3` but NOT the
   differently-named `results/arc_e3_origin_fixtures`, so ruff linted them. Added, with
   the rationale in-file. Verified through the REAL hook path (`pre-commit run ruff
   --files <fixture>` -> Passed) rather than a direct CLI call, which bypasses excludes
   unless `--force-exclude` is passed -- the official hook supplies it, my manual
   invocation did not, and that difference made the fix look broken when it was not.

ALSO FIXED: A LINT THAT CONTRADICTED THE PROJECT'S OWN STANDARD.
`arc_artifact_lint` refused exp6011's `complete_four_arm_matrix_measured` as a
NON_TERMINAL_HONEST_VERDICT. CLAUDE.md's Verdict Terminal-Prefix Discipline lists EIGHT
accepted prefixes -- both the colon AND the underscore form of each -- but
`arc_solve_artifact_discipline.TERMINAL_VERDICT_PREFIXES` carried only the four colon
forms. The underscore form is not merely permitted but often SAFER: a literal ": " in a
verdict is what caused the research-complete.yaml colon-poison incident, which cascaded
into a pretest SKIP of an entire milestone. So the lint was pushing authors toward the
riskier form. Widened the tuple rather than rewriting the artifact's verdict -- the
artifact was already CLAUDE.md-compliant, and editing a measurement's verdict to satisfy
a stricter-than-spec checker is bending the evidence to fit the tool. This also removes a
disagreement between two gates meant to encode one rule: the conductor's
`_verdict_is_untrustworthy` classifier already accepted the underscore forms. Verified the
widened tuple still REJECTS blocked_*/partial_*/marginal_*/telemetry_claim_blocked/empty/
None -- 10/10 cases, no mismatches.

FINAL DIFF over all 11 rebuilt artifacts: ZERO genuinely-changed values (clock fields
and purely-additive keys excluded), every verdict identical.

Verify: arc_orphan_solver_lint OK (65 modules) · determination_preservation_lint OK ·
artifact_freshness_lint OK · arc_llm_on_liveness_lint --self-test OK (8/8 origin
cells, 16 mutation proofs) · 96 passed across the related suites. The 5 skips are
PRE-EXISTING and unrelated -- module-level importorskip for missing `pulp`/`onnx`
plus one documented import-side-effect hang (.48 RETRO); they fire at COLLECTION
time, before -k filtering, so they are collateral rather than part of this selection.

No SUBMITTED_* flag flipped. MAX_ACTIONS (400) and SUBMITTED_EARLY_STOP_GRACE (None)
untouched. Nothing submitted to ARC or Kaggle -- external submission is operator-only.

